up at 3:00 AM in the morning?

00:04:12.930 --> 00:04:15.100
No, I don't think that's it.

00:04:15.100 --> 00:04:20.500
We know the benefits, and we
have the best of intentions.

00:04:20.500 --> 00:04:22.290
And then reality hits.

00:04:22.290 --> 00:04:24.300
The more tests we
add, the more we

00:04:24.300 --> 00:04:29.270
realize that it takes time and
resources to run these tests.

00:04:29.270 --> 00:04:31.680
And it becomes a drag on
developer productivity.

00:04:31.680 --> 00:04:35.130
It's very common to kick
off a build, and then

00:04:35.130 --> 00:04:36.990
head out to lunch, right?

00:04:36.990 --> 00:04:38.570
And then you come back, and--

00:04:38.570 --> 00:04:40.750
OK, build's not done.

00:04:40.750 --> 00:04:42.330
Let's go get some coffee.

00:04:42.330 --> 00:04:43.920
All right, came back.

00:04:43.920 --> 00:04:45.705
All right, still going.

00:04:45.705 --> 00:04:47.889
I'm at 67%.

00:04:47.889 --> 00:04:49.430
All right, I guess
it's beer o'clock.

00:04:49.430 --> 00:04:50.760
All right, here we go.

00:04:50.760 --> 00:04:53.100
Eventually, though, you
want to actually get back

00:04:53.100 --> 00:04:54.120
to writing code.

00:04:54.120 --> 00:04:55.480
So what do we do?

00:04:55.480 --> 00:04:56.909
We take shortcuts.

00:04:56.909 --> 00:04:58.200
We turn down our test coverage.

00:04:58.200 --> 00:04:59.310
We run them less often.

00:04:59.310 --> 00:05:01.197
And then that leads to bit rot.

00:05:01.197 --> 00:05:02.280
Don't even write the test.

00:05:02.280 --> 00:05:04.238
Why write them if you're
not going to run them?

00:05:04.238 --> 00:05:06.240
And this is why we're
not testing at the level

00:05:06.240 --> 00:05:10.080
that we feel we should be.

00:05:10.080 --> 00:05:12.822
We end up in this
conundrum here.

00:05:12.822 --> 00:05:14.280
As you step up your
test practices,

00:05:14.280 --> 00:05:15.984
your code gets
better and better.

00:05:15.984 --> 00:05:17.400
But your tests
take longer to run,

00:05:17.400 --> 00:05:19.590
and you need more
and more hardware.

00:05:19.590 --> 00:05:21.450
And that's a tough choice.

00:05:21.450 --> 00:05:23.640
What's the sweet spot in here?

00:05:23.640 --> 00:05:26.580
We can test more and more, and
take longer to get our code out

00:05:26.580 --> 00:05:29.130
to production, or we can
test less, and get it out

00:05:29.130 --> 00:05:29.860
there sooner.

00:05:29.860 --> 00:05:33.139
It's we can ship it now,
or we can ship it right.

00:05:33.139 --> 00:05:34.680
I don't know how to
make that choice,

00:05:34.680 --> 00:05:36.304
and other people
struggle with it, too.

00:05:36.304 --> 00:05:39.780
In our study, we found that the
number one concern among teams

00:05:39.780 --> 00:05:43.620
with sophisticated test practice
is balancing test frequency

00:05:43.620 --> 00:05:47.200
with the resources required
to run those tests.

00:05:47.200 --> 00:05:49.830
So what if we could
flatten that curve?

00:05:49.830 --> 00:05:52.350
Get to a place where we can
have more and more tests,

00:05:52.350 --> 00:05:54.120
and run them more
and more frequently,

00:05:54.120 --> 00:05:57.480
without incurring
increasing overhead

00:05:57.480 --> 00:06:00.910
that spirals out of control.

00:06:00.910 --> 00:06:03.380
Well, hello, Bazel.

00:06:03.380 --> 00:06:06.120
Bazel is Google's build system.

00:06:06.120 --> 00:06:07.860
It's been used by
nearly every one

00:06:07.860 --> 00:06:10.200
of Google's thousands of
engineers-- tens of thousands

00:06:10.200 --> 00:06:12.060
of engineers--
for over 10 years,

00:06:12.060 --> 00:06:15.120
and it's available as
free, open source software.

00:06:15.120 --> 00:06:19.680
At Google, we have an intense
commitment to testing.

00:06:19.680 --> 00:06:23.520
We exercise and validate every
code path on every code change.

00:06:23.520 --> 00:06:28.460
And most teams maintain a very
high level of code coverage.

00:06:28.460 --> 00:06:31.942
As we grew, we ended up
running, as all of us

00:06:31.942 --> 00:06:32.900
do, into this problem--

00:06:32.900 --> 00:06:36.050
the conundrum of more
testing meant more time.

00:06:36.050 --> 00:06:39.320
We didn't want to slow down
and ship software less often.

00:06:39.320 --> 00:06:41.040
We didn't want to
ship more bugs.

00:06:41.040 --> 00:06:47.090
So we created Bazel to allow
us to test all the things

00:06:47.090 --> 00:06:48.680
without losing that velocity.

00:06:48.680 --> 00:06:53.210
To do so, Bazel was designed
with two major optimizations

00:06:53.210 --> 00:06:56.730
that allows us to run our build
and test more efficiently.

00:06:56.730 --> 00:06:58.820
So the first is,
don't repeat yourself.

00:06:58.820 --> 00:07:01.640
If you've already run something,
and it hasn't changed,

00:07:01.640 --> 00:07:03.260
why run it again?

00:07:03.260 --> 00:07:05.900
By reusing the prior
results, it means that we

00:07:05.900 --> 00:07:09.920
can scale our build and test--

00:07:09.920 --> 00:07:12.040
as our builds and tests
scale exponentially,

00:07:12.040 --> 00:07:14.030
our time doesn't have
to scale that way,

00:07:14.030 --> 00:07:16.220
because we're only going
to retest and re-exercise

00:07:16.220 --> 00:07:18.500
the parts that have changed.

00:07:18.500 --> 00:07:22.540
The second major optimization is
to build and work in parallel.

00:07:22.540 --> 00:07:25.270
So build actions, and
especially tests, are generally

00:07:25.270 --> 00:07:26.770
very parallelizable.

00:07:26.770 --> 00:07:29.260
You might build one binary,
and run a whole bunch

00:07:29.260 --> 00:07:31.310
of independent tests on it.

00:07:31.310 --> 00:07:33.280
And so by having
this fanout ability,

00:07:33.280 --> 00:07:35.650
we can run across
modern multicore systems

00:07:35.650 --> 00:07:38.850
and get maximum efficiency.

00:07:38.850 --> 00:07:40.970
What can you build with Bazel?

00:07:40.970 --> 00:07:42.590
Well, just about everything.

00:07:42.590 --> 00:07:44.720
Bazel provides an
extensibility mechanism

00:07:44.720 --> 00:07:47.240
where you use a simple,
Python-like language

00:07:47.240 --> 00:07:48.170
to write plugins.

00:07:48.170 --> 00:07:50.960
We call them build rules
for various language.

00:07:50.960 --> 00:07:53.017
This is just a partial
list of the set

00:07:53.017 --> 00:07:55.100
of languages that have
been built by our community

00:07:55.100 --> 00:07:57.200
to extend Bazel.

00:07:57.200 --> 00:07:58.640
I've shown this
slide many times,

00:07:58.640 --> 00:08:01.160
and I was at a conference
recently, showing it off.

00:08:01.160 --> 00:08:02.724
And someone came
up to me and said,

00:08:02.724 --> 00:08:04.640
all right, you've got a
lot of stuff in there,

00:08:04.640 --> 00:08:07.580
but what about OCaml.

00:08:07.580 --> 00:08:08.949
And I said, I don't know.

00:08:08.949 --> 00:08:09.490
I don't know.

00:08:09.490 --> 00:08:11.870
Went to my phone and
googled it, and found

00:08:11.870 --> 00:08:13.420
out, oh, right, OCaml.

00:08:13.420 --> 00:08:14.690
We have that.

00:08:14.690 --> 00:08:16.640
So there's OCaml.

00:08:16.640 --> 00:08:18.410
We actually play a
game at conferences

00:08:18.410 --> 00:08:20.000
sometimes called
Stump the Bazel.

00:08:20.000 --> 00:08:21.230
See if you can come
up with a language

00:08:21.230 --> 00:08:22.920
that we don't have
bulit-in support for.

00:08:22.920 --> 00:08:26.870
And if you do, it's
pretty easy to write.

00:08:26.870 --> 00:08:29.360
You can use Bazel across
your entire organization

00:08:29.360 --> 00:08:34.190
for every language that you're
working in today and tomorrow.

00:08:34.190 --> 00:08:36.470
Let's take a look
at how Bazel works.

00:08:36.470 --> 00:08:38.929
So first, consider a
traditional approach.

00:08:38.929 --> 00:08:41.570
We're going to try and
build this code base.

00:08:41.570 --> 00:08:43.700
This is a portion of the
TensorFlow code base.

00:08:43.700 --> 00:08:45.740
It's actually much,
much more than that.

00:08:45.740 --> 00:08:48.140
And our goal is to get to
an executable, something

00:08:48.140 --> 00:08:49.310
that we can run.

00:08:49.310 --> 00:08:52.610
So the traditional build process
works something like this.

00:08:52.610 --> 00:08:56.700
First, we take all the
things, and then we

00:08:56.700 --> 00:08:59.440
do this thing, and that
thing, and the other thing.

00:08:59.440 --> 00:09:01.980
And then we cross our
fingers, and hopefully we

00:09:01.980 --> 00:09:04.152
get something out that can run.

00:09:04.152 --> 00:09:06.360
Now, there's two problems
with what I just described.

00:09:06.360 --> 00:09:08.940
One is all the things.

00:09:08.940 --> 00:09:10.710
Maybe we don't need
all those things

00:09:10.710 --> 00:09:14.040
for this particular
thing that we're creating

00:09:14.040 --> 00:09:17.280
and two is I said this, and
then that, and then the other.

00:09:17.280 --> 00:09:20.010
This sounds very
serial, and I think

00:09:20.010 --> 00:09:23.680
we know that modern software
is all about parallel.

00:09:23.680 --> 00:09:26.502
So with Bazel, we took
a different approach.

00:09:26.502 --> 00:09:28.710
The first thing we noticed
is that source code is not

00:09:28.710 --> 00:09:30.900
a bunch of random stuff.

00:09:30.900 --> 00:09:32.160
OK, mine sometimes is.

00:09:32.160 --> 00:09:34.830
But eventually I figure
it out, and what I do

00:09:34.830 --> 00:09:38.610
is I write source code that is
an interrelated set of highly

00:09:38.610 --> 00:09:40.800
structured symbols.

00:09:40.800 --> 00:09:43.350
So with Bazel, using a
declarative configuration

00:09:43.350 --> 00:09:48.360
language, we define the units of
code and how they interoperate.

00:09:48.360 --> 00:09:51.090
Bazel interprets that
to create actions,

00:09:51.090 --> 00:09:56.660
and each action has fully
specified inputs and outputs.

00:09:56.660 --> 00:10:01.480
These add up to a unique
signature for that action.

00:10:01.480 --> 00:10:04.780
Each of these fully
specified action

00:10:04.780 --> 00:10:08.205
is independent and can be
individually addressed.

00:10:08.205 --> 00:10:10.330
We know everything that
goes into it and everything

00:10:10.330 --> 00:10:12.860
that we expect to
come out of it.

00:10:12.860 --> 00:10:16.340
We composite these into a graph,
where the output of one action

00:10:16.340 --> 00:10:19.810
can become an input
into the next.

00:10:19.810 --> 00:10:22.360
And these actions can be cached.

00:10:22.360 --> 00:10:24.540
If the inputs to an
action don't change,

00:10:24.540 --> 00:10:26.610
then we don't need to
regenerate the output.

00:10:31.190 --> 00:10:32.690
All right, let's zoom out.

00:10:32.690 --> 00:10:37.460
This is a graph of Bazel's build
graph, a directed acyclic graph

00:10:37.460 --> 00:10:40.496
of all of the steps and the
flow to get from your inputs

00:10:40.496 --> 00:10:41.120
to your output.

00:10:41.120 --> 00:10:43.480
This represents
a very small one.

00:10:43.480 --> 00:10:45.230
So let's say we're
going to make a change.

00:10:45.230 --> 00:10:48.655
We'll make a change to
this file and that file.

00:10:48.655 --> 00:10:50.030
Well, everything
upstream of that

00:10:50.030 --> 00:10:52.850
is going to need to be rebuilt.
So we'll invalidate the cache

00:10:52.850 --> 00:10:54.356
for all of these bits.

00:10:54.356 --> 00:10:55.730
There's a fair
amount to do here,

00:10:55.730 --> 00:10:58.330
but notice there's a whole
lot that we don't have to do.

00:10:58.330 --> 00:11:00.200
And because of
Bazel's build graph,

00:11:00.200 --> 00:11:02.090
we know for sure that
we don't have to do it,

00:11:02.090 --> 00:11:04.360
and we're going to
get the right output.

00:11:04.360 --> 00:11:06.290
The other thing we can
see is that this area

00:11:06.290 --> 00:11:10.190
of the graph and this
area have no common edges.

00:11:10.190 --> 00:11:13.739
And that means we can
execute them in parallel.

00:11:13.739 --> 00:11:14.780
Now we're ready to build.

00:11:14.780 --> 00:11:18.120
We rock the graph and
arrive at our output.

00:11:18.120 --> 00:11:20.010
So putting it all together.

00:11:20.010 --> 00:11:24.080
When I say, Bazel, let's
build and test this,

00:11:24.080 --> 00:11:26.430
Bazel's going to
pull in the inputs,

00:11:26.430 --> 00:11:30.780
generate that build graph,
then consult the cache

00:11:30.780 --> 00:11:33.930
to see if there's
anything we can reuse,

00:11:33.930 --> 00:11:37.020
prune out everything
that's already been built,

00:11:37.020 --> 00:11:40.500
then distribute that work across
all the cores on my machine

00:11:40.500 --> 00:11:45.802
for parallel execution
and rapid delivery.

00:11:45.802 --> 00:11:47.010
All right, let's take a look.

