00:14:01.290 --> 00:14:02.820
and go ahead and run through it.

00:14:02.820 --> 00:14:04.480
Then we'll rerun in Bazel.

00:14:04.480 --> 00:14:07.760
Bazel, test all.

00:14:07.760 --> 00:14:10.570
So Bazel is going to
analyze all my files,

00:14:10.570 --> 00:14:13.839
compile them, and
run in parallel.

00:14:13.839 --> 00:14:15.880
Forgot to mention what
I'm running this thing on.

00:14:15.880 --> 00:14:19.780
This is too GCE VMs,
16-core machines--

00:14:19.780 --> 00:14:22.210
identical machines--
running Maven on the left,

00:14:22.210 --> 00:14:23.640
and Bazel on the right.

00:14:23.640 --> 00:14:25.390
Bazel ran in parallel
and, as you can see,

00:14:25.390 --> 00:14:28.750
very rapidly found
that I have an error.

00:14:28.750 --> 00:14:31.195
Oh, my error's in my test.

00:14:31.195 --> 00:14:32.570
So we'll go ahead--
actually, I'm

00:14:32.570 --> 00:14:34.610
going to wait for Maven
to continue, to finish.

00:14:34.610 --> 00:14:36.670
Maven's running through
all of its tests,

00:14:36.670 --> 00:14:39.310
taking a little longer because
it's not parallelizing.

00:14:39.310 --> 00:14:42.448
Still doing all the work.

00:14:42.448 --> 00:14:48.330
And it should be
just about done here.

00:14:48.330 --> 00:14:49.040
There it is.

00:14:49.040 --> 00:14:51.390
And they found the same failure.

00:14:51.390 --> 00:14:53.730
All right, so the
failure's in my test class.

00:14:53.730 --> 00:14:59.230
Let's go ahead and edit vim,
source, test, Java, blah,

00:14:59.230 --> 00:15:00.280
blah, blah.

00:15:00.280 --> 00:15:04.200
And I have a greeter test,
and this funny stuff again.

00:15:04.200 --> 00:15:07.738
Go ahead and edit anyway.

00:15:07.738 --> 00:15:09.240
All right, and what did we say?

00:15:09.240 --> 00:15:12.110
We said Hello Nexties.

00:15:12.110 --> 00:15:13.340
That's catching on, right?

00:15:13.340 --> 00:15:14.548
We're calling ourselves that?

00:15:17.420 --> 00:15:19.735
Nexties.

00:15:19.735 --> 00:15:22.710
All right, and we'll save that.

00:15:22.710 --> 00:15:25.842
And now we have it on this one.

00:15:25.842 --> 00:15:26.550
So what do we do?

00:15:26.550 --> 00:15:28.410
We say just continue, right?

00:15:28.410 --> 00:15:32.470
All right, and then we're going
to rerun our build and test

00:15:32.470 --> 00:15:35.810
action, Maven on the
left, Bazel on the right.

00:15:35.810 --> 00:15:39.340
So maven's kicking
off, and run-- oh.

00:15:39.340 --> 00:15:42.970
I've failed somehow.

00:15:42.970 --> 00:15:47.060
But as we can see, Bazel
did it much faster.

00:15:47.060 --> 00:15:49.664
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:15:49.664 --> 00:15:51.330
DAVE STANKE: See,
this is a coding test.

00:15:51.330 --> 00:15:53.905
Thank you.

00:15:53.905 --> 00:15:54.405
Success.

00:15:57.030 --> 00:15:59.150
And go ahead and capital that.

00:16:03.970 --> 00:16:05.440
Rerun.

00:16:05.440 --> 00:16:09.490
It's only going to rerun
that one test that changed.

00:16:09.490 --> 00:16:12.830
And we passed in a very
short amount of time.

00:16:17.330 --> 00:16:21.340
Whoops, what button was that?

00:16:21.340 --> 00:16:23.754
All right, so to recap with
Bazel, we made a change.

00:16:23.754 --> 00:16:25.170
Bazel's going to
look at its cache

00:16:25.170 --> 00:16:29.060
and not bother rebuilding
anything it doesn't need to.

00:16:29.060 --> 00:16:31.870
Then it's going to fan out and
use all the cores on my machine

00:16:31.870 --> 00:16:35.020
to build in parallel.

00:16:35.020 --> 00:16:38.795
Now, I was running this
demo on 16-core machines.

00:16:42.050 --> 00:16:44.600
Do you use a 16-core
machine every day?

00:16:44.600 --> 00:16:45.170
Maybe you do.

00:16:45.170 --> 00:16:45.730
I don't.

00:16:45.730 --> 00:16:48.740
I use this little
laptop with two cores.

00:16:48.740 --> 00:16:51.800
And sure, you can give
all of your developers

00:16:51.800 --> 00:16:53.162
16-core machines.

00:16:53.162 --> 00:16:55.370
And most of the time, they're
going to be real happy.

00:16:55.370 --> 00:16:59.420
Running and IDE doesn't take
a whole lot of horsepower.

00:16:59.420 --> 00:17:01.430
And if we're doing
a modest build

00:17:01.430 --> 00:17:03.350
process, still pretty good.

00:17:03.350 --> 00:17:07.730
It's a little wasteful, but
everything is working fine.

00:17:07.730 --> 00:17:09.619
But eventually, these
machines are going

00:17:09.619 --> 00:17:12.069
to start to look underpowered.

00:17:12.069 --> 00:17:14.450
This is because build
is intrinsically

00:17:14.450 --> 00:17:18.500
a spiky workload, which means
our developer machines go

00:17:18.500 --> 00:17:22.040
from being overpowered
to underpowered.

00:17:22.040 --> 00:17:24.950
And yes, even with
Bazel, because Bazel

00:17:24.950 --> 00:17:28.099
can exercise all of the
cores on one machine,

00:17:28.099 --> 00:17:32.410
but it can't conjure up
new cores out of thin air.

00:17:32.410 --> 00:17:35.080
Or can it?

00:17:35.080 --> 00:17:37.060
Remember, each of
these Bazel actions

00:17:37.060 --> 00:17:41.600
is an independent
unit of execution.

00:17:41.600 --> 00:17:44.300
Which is how we can cache
so aggressively and shard

00:17:44.300 --> 00:17:47.400
across all the
cores of a machine.

00:17:47.400 --> 00:17:51.050
So how about we tap into
the cloud and get a bunch

00:17:51.050 --> 00:17:52.340
more cores?

00:17:52.340 --> 00:17:55.750
We can scale way beyond
a single machine.

00:17:55.750 --> 00:17:57.700
And everyone in
the team can share

00:17:57.700 --> 00:17:59.320
that pool of worker cores.

00:17:59.320 --> 00:18:01.930
So we can bin pack
those spiky workloads.

00:18:01.930 --> 00:18:04.870
When I need a build, I'm going
to grab a bunch of cores.

00:18:04.870 --> 00:18:08.200
And when I'm done, you can
grab a bunch of course.

00:18:08.200 --> 00:18:10.630
And we can scale the
total pool to whatever

00:18:10.630 --> 00:18:13.200
we need for our team.

00:18:13.200 --> 00:18:16.360
Also, we can move our
cache up into the cloud.

00:18:16.360 --> 00:18:19.350
So if anyone on my team has
previously built something,

00:18:19.350 --> 00:18:22.552
then everyone gets
to reuse that work.

00:18:22.552 --> 00:18:24.760
This is great for getting
new team members onboarded.

00:18:24.760 --> 00:18:27.000
A first build for
a new team member

00:18:27.000 --> 00:18:28.270
isn't starting from scratch.

00:18:28.270 --> 00:18:29.910
It's starting from
whatever the build

00:18:29.910 --> 00:18:32.130
was that the experienced
person made yesterday,

00:18:32.130 --> 00:18:34.720
or this morning.

00:18:34.720 --> 00:18:37.170
Today, I am excited to
introduce Remote Build

00:18:37.170 --> 00:18:38.994
Execution for Bazel.

00:18:38.994 --> 00:18:41.160
It's a Google Cloud Platform
service, which has just

00:18:41.160 --> 00:18:43.380
been launched to alpha.

00:18:43.380 --> 00:18:45.630
It's inspired by the services
that we use internally

00:18:45.630 --> 00:18:48.390
at Google, which are a big part
of supporting our engineering

00:18:48.390 --> 00:18:51.500
culture and commitment to tests.

00:18:51.500 --> 00:18:54.170
It's based on open
source APIs developed

00:18:54.170 --> 00:18:55.700
by the Bazel community.

00:18:55.700 --> 00:18:58.190
This is a fully managed
Google Cloud Service,

00:18:58.190 --> 00:19:02.510
provides scalable worker
pools for massively parallel

00:19:02.510 --> 00:19:05.300
build and test workloads,
and a shared cache

00:19:05.300 --> 00:19:08.060
that every build client,
whether a developer or CI, can

00:19:08.060 --> 00:19:09.310
leverage.

00:19:09.310 --> 00:19:11.960
Let's see it in action.

00:19:11.960 --> 00:19:15.140
So we're going to build
that same application

00:19:15.140 --> 00:19:17.702
that we looked at earlier.

00:19:17.702 --> 00:19:20.160
But this time, we're going to
go into the TensorFlow source

00:19:20.160 --> 00:19:20.880
code.

00:19:20.880 --> 00:19:22.890
TensorFlow is a huge project.

00:19:22.890 --> 00:19:30.910
Massive C++ source code, tens
of thousands of files and tests.

00:19:30.910 --> 00:19:33.160
Let's make a change.

00:19:33.160 --> 00:19:36.130
So, vim.

00:19:36.130 --> 00:19:38.870
If we go into the
core here live,

00:19:38.870 --> 00:19:41.560
there's some string libraries.

00:19:41.560 --> 00:19:46.560
And here's a file within there.

00:19:46.560 --> 00:19:51.115
And we're going to modify
this global identifier.

00:19:51.115 --> 00:19:51.860
Let's see.

00:19:54.840 --> 00:19:57.660
So what should we have
our global identifier be?

00:19:57.660 --> 00:20:01.050
Let's go with
Hello-- oh, all caps.

00:20:01.050 --> 00:20:02.900
Didn't mean that.

00:20:02.900 --> 00:20:05.615
Hello Nexties.

00:20:05.615 --> 00:20:07.270
I feel like I heard
someone saying.

00:20:07.270 --> 00:20:09.835
It's like, what people
are calling the attendees.

00:20:13.300 --> 00:20:17.550
And go ahead and
quit out of that.

00:20:17.550 --> 00:20:21.150
And then on the left
side, we'll go ahead

00:20:21.150 --> 00:20:24.430
and rebuild with Bazel.

00:20:24.430 --> 00:20:28.230
And of course, it's going to
run through all of its inputs,

00:20:28.230 --> 00:20:29.980
and figure out what
work needs to be done,

00:20:29.980 --> 00:20:32.770
and distribute that
across all 16 cores.

00:20:32.770 --> 00:20:35.046
OK, there's 7,000
or so different bits

00:20:35.046 --> 00:20:35.920
that it needs to run.

00:20:35.920 --> 00:20:38.710
It's actually going to
find a few more as it runs.

00:20:38.710 --> 00:20:42.070
On the left, we'll
run that same thing.

00:20:42.070 --> 00:20:45.145
Test, colon all-- oops--

00:20:45.145 --> 00:20:46.180
all.

00:20:46.180 --> 00:20:50.260
This time, I'm going to use
a configuration that includes

00:20:50.260 --> 00:20:51.540
remote build execution.

00:20:55.330 --> 00:20:59.490
Do that same analysis, and start
running, and oh my gosh, look.

00:20:59.490 --> 00:21:03.160
We are running on 600 cores.

00:21:03.160 --> 00:21:04.750
So this work is
being distributed

00:21:04.750 --> 00:21:08.680
to 600 individual
workers in the cloud.

00:21:08.680 --> 00:21:11.657
This is, again, using that
open source protocol for remote

00:21:11.657 --> 00:21:12.490
build distributions.

00:21:12.490 --> 00:21:14.950
Based on gRPC and Protobuf.

00:21:14.950 --> 00:21:19.960
And it uses docker containers to
provide an isolation mechanism.

00:21:19.960 --> 00:21:23.620
The container is where
your work gets executed,

00:21:23.620 --> 00:21:27.700
and that's part of the input
to the build that's happening.

00:21:27.700 --> 00:21:30.790
And it's executed in our
fully managed service that

00:21:30.790 --> 00:21:33.290
carries the same commitment
to performance, reliability,

00:21:33.290 --> 00:21:37.670
and security that you've
come to expect from GCP.

00:21:37.670 --> 00:21:40.420
So now, another benefit
of working in the cloud

00:21:40.420 --> 00:21:44.200
is that we can send our
outputs to a shared place.

00:21:44.200 --> 00:21:47.170
So I want to show you a cool
additional feature, which

00:21:47.170 --> 00:21:52.330
is this UI for build results.

00:21:52.330 --> 00:21:54.980
This is representing the
build I'm doing right now.

00:21:54.980 --> 00:21:56.730
You can see a list
here of all of the work

00:21:56.730 --> 00:21:58.330
that Bazel has
decided it needs to do

00:21:58.330 --> 00:22:01.150
and is currently
running through.

00:22:01.150 --> 00:22:03.250
And a successful rate,
and some of them that

00:22:03.250 --> 00:22:04.490
haven't happened yet.

00:22:04.490 --> 00:22:05.980
We've got our invocation log.

00:22:05.980 --> 00:22:08.060
Here's a full list of
the invocation details.

00:22:08.060 --> 00:22:10.214
This is what Bazel
is going to do.

00:22:10.214 --> 00:22:12.630
And we can get artifacts, the
things that Bazel has built.

00:22:12.630 --> 00:22:15.940
We can go ahead and download
our intermediate libraries

00:22:15.940 --> 00:22:18.230
and things straight
out of the cloud.

00:22:18.230 --> 00:22:20.810
This is available to
everyone on your team.

00:22:20.810 --> 00:22:23.110
So if I'm having a
problem building,

00:22:23.110 --> 00:22:26.050
I can go to my overview page.

00:22:26.050 --> 00:22:28.480
I can take this
link, and copy that,

00:22:28.480 --> 00:22:30.040
and send it to a colleague.

00:22:30.040 --> 00:22:32.020
And they can understand
my build and the

00:22:32.020 --> 00:22:34.990
needs that I have without
having to redownload and rebuild

00:22:34.990 --> 00:22:36.520
everything themselves.

00:22:36.520 --> 00:22:39.220
Or they can go ahead
and rebuild based

00:22:39.220 --> 00:22:41.490
on knowledge of
exactly what Bazel

00:22:41.490 --> 00:22:45.546
build I did from this input.

00:22:45.546 --> 00:22:47.920
Let's go ahead and refresh
and see how we're doing on our

00:22:47.920 --> 00:22:48.840
build.

00:22:48.840 --> 00:22:52.720
Looks like we're getting
pretty close here.

00:22:52.720 --> 00:22:53.590
Check back in.

00:22:56.330 --> 00:22:59.040
All right, now we've come down
to a bit of a pinch point.

00:22:59.040 --> 00:23:00.915
And you can see that
we're doing our linking.

00:23:00.915 --> 00:23:02.880
That tends to be a bottleneck.

00:23:02.880 --> 00:23:06.086
We're still distributing
across dozens or hundreds

00:23:06.086 --> 00:23:07.710
of individual nodes--
a lot more than I

00:23:07.710 --> 00:23:09.030
could process on one team.

00:23:09.030 --> 00:23:11.196
All right, and we've started
running into the tests.

00:23:11.196 --> 00:23:15.570
And oops, I have a test failure.

00:23:15.570 --> 00:23:18.690
Using this UI, that's going
to pop right to the top.

00:23:18.690 --> 00:23:20.520
Here's my failing target.

00:23:20.520 --> 00:23:23.400
Click into my strings
test, and what do we find?

00:23:23.400 --> 00:23:29.110
Oh, right, I was supposed to
write Hello Nexties in my test,

00:23:29.110 --> 00:23:33.675
but instead it still
says TensorFlow 1.9.

00:23:33.675 --> 00:23:36.050
Again, this is a great way to
collaborate with teammates.

00:23:36.050 --> 00:23:38.390
Send this to someone who
understands this test suite,

00:23:38.390 --> 00:23:40.550
and they can help
me understand it.

00:23:40.550 --> 00:23:43.430
I'm not a C++ expert, but in
this case it's fortunately

00:23:43.430 --> 00:23:47.300
pretty clear what I'm
supposed to do about this.

00:23:47.300 --> 00:23:49.370
Pop back over.

00:23:49.370 --> 00:23:54.052
Still running off a few
additional finalized bits.

00:23:54.052 --> 00:23:58.220
Final testing, and there we go.

00:23:58.220 --> 00:24:00.520
We see a summary of all
the things that passed,

00:24:00.520 --> 00:24:02.210
and that one test that failed.

00:24:02.210 --> 00:24:04.090
I'm going to go ahead
and make an edit there.

00:24:04.090 --> 00:24:16.810
And let's see, it's under
lib, strings, numbers, test.

00:24:16.810 --> 00:24:23.120
And this time, we'll get
the capitalization correct.

00:24:23.120 --> 00:24:25.710
So it was-- here, you know what?

00:24:25.710 --> 00:24:29.070
How about we copy and paste?

00:24:29.070 --> 00:24:30.480
No fat fingering here.

00:24:33.366 --> 00:24:38.100
And go ahead and
paste that in, save,

00:24:38.100 --> 00:24:41.430
and I'm going to
rerun my test suite.

00:24:41.430 --> 00:24:44.640
This time, the only change I
made was to that one test file.

00:24:44.640 --> 00:24:48.410
We're going to recompile
the test, which

00:24:48.410 --> 00:24:49.965
is running again on the remote.

00:24:49.965 --> 00:24:52.530
Going to relink that
to single action,

00:24:52.530 --> 00:24:56.300
so it's running in single
threaded mode effectively.

00:24:56.300 --> 00:24:57.260
And rerun.

00:24:57.260 --> 00:25:01.040
And what you'll see is it cached
all of those previous results,

00:25:01.040 --> 00:25:04.510
and only reran the one
test that I needed to run.

00:25:04.510 --> 00:25:06.690
Again, this works
across your whole team.

00:25:06.690 --> 00:25:09.050
So if I'm the one who
made the original error,

00:25:09.050 --> 00:25:10.580
and my colleague
made the change,

00:25:10.580 --> 00:25:12.580
they're going to inherit
all of the test results

00:25:12.580 --> 00:25:13.850
that I got in the past.

00:25:13.850 --> 00:25:15.740
This is really great
for making people

00:25:15.740 --> 00:25:18.770
on your team
effective, fast, happy.

00:25:22.090 --> 00:25:25.600
So to sum up, we
used a shared cache

00:25:25.600 --> 00:25:28.780
to de-duplicate across
your entire team.

00:25:28.780 --> 00:25:30.820
The shared worker pool
means I can parallelize

00:25:30.820 --> 00:25:33.010
to the scale of the cloud.

00:25:33.010 --> 00:25:35.650
And cloud-hosted UI
improves our effectiveness

00:25:35.650 --> 00:25:40.510
as a team, communication,
and joint debugging.

00:25:40.510 --> 00:25:41.822
Now, Cloud Build.

00:25:41.822 --> 00:25:43.030
This was announced this week.

00:25:43.030 --> 00:25:45.470
Hopefully you've been
hearing about this.

00:25:45.470 --> 00:25:48.640
It's a fully managed
CICD system on GCP,

00:25:48.640 --> 00:25:51.610
and it has out-of-the-box
support for Bazel built in.

00:25:51.610 --> 00:25:53.260
There's a supported
build step, which

00:25:53.260 --> 00:25:56.020
makes running Bazel workloads
and cloud build really simple--

00:25:56.020 --> 00:25:57.977
just a couple lines of code.

00:25:57.977 --> 00:25:59.560
And it's got a
Bazel-optimized results

00:25:59.560 --> 00:26:02.120
UI for collaborative
troubleshooting.

00:26:02.120 --> 00:26:06.670
So you can have Cloud
Build driving your process

00:26:06.670 --> 00:26:11.350
as part of a CI system,
getting that same shared

00:26:11.350 --> 00:26:14.880
cache and same shared
giant cloud compute pool.

00:26:17.410 --> 00:26:21.130
Using RBE for your CI builds
enables you to fully test

00:26:21.130 --> 00:26:22.092
every single change.

00:26:22.092 --> 00:26:24.550
From one commit to the next,
typically these are all small,

00:26:24.550 --> 00:26:26.110
incremental changes.

00:26:26.110 --> 00:26:28.180
And so then the same is
true of pull requests,

00:26:28.180 --> 00:26:31.600
and even developer builds.

00:26:31.600 --> 00:26:34.390
Using the shared cache
in the distributed pool

00:26:34.390 --> 00:26:37.030
means that every change can be
tested, because everyone's only

00:26:37.030 --> 00:26:39.860
going to reperform the amount
of work that it needs to do.

00:26:39.860 --> 00:26:41.760
So the time it takes
to run your test suite

00:26:41.760 --> 00:26:44.610
is proportional to the
amount of change made,

00:26:44.610 --> 00:26:47.804
not to the total size
of the code base.

00:26:47.804 --> 00:26:49.220
With Remote Build
Execution, we've

00:26:49.220 --> 00:26:52.100
been partnering with several
teams, Google projects, and GCP

00:26:52.100 --> 00:26:55.700
customers as part of an
early access program for RBE.

00:26:55.700 --> 00:26:57.790
And these partners have
seen great improvements

00:26:57.790 --> 00:26:59.349
in build speed and reliability.

00:26:59.349 --> 00:27:01.640
Some of the workloads our
customers are running include

00:27:01.640 --> 00:27:05.390
Scala web services,
C++ system libraries,

00:27:05.390 --> 00:27:08.330
and IDEs written in Java,
where we're doing full headless

00:27:08.330 --> 00:27:12.830
testing of those interactive
environments in the nodes

00:27:12.830 --> 00:27:15.770
on RBE.

00:27:15.770 --> 00:27:19.220
One of those customers is the
TensorFlow team themselves.

00:27:19.220 --> 00:27:23.516
They built their very large
polyglot project on RBE.

00:27:23.516 --> 00:27:25.890
And they've been piloting
Remote Built Execution with us,

00:27:25.890 --> 00:27:28.550
and have achieved a 5x
improvement in their

00:27:28.550 --> 00:27:29.660
build speed.

00:27:29.660 --> 00:27:33.140
That's coupled to a 5x reduction
in the amount of machinery

00:27:33.140 --> 00:27:35.070
necessary to do these workloads.

00:27:35.070 --> 00:27:36.770
So 5x plus 5x--

00:27:36.770 --> 00:27:38.574
I don't know if
that's a 10x or a 25x,

00:27:38.574 --> 00:27:41.240
but it's a lot of x's, and we're
really excited about what we're

00:27:41.240 --> 00:27:42.920
being able to achieve here.

00:27:42.920 --> 00:27:46.640
They used to have a long
backlog of pull request testing.

00:27:46.640 --> 00:27:48.170
They had this one
poor engineer who

00:27:48.170 --> 00:27:52.040
had to very carefully
mete out the test

00:27:52.040 --> 00:27:54.350
against this fragile
infrastructure.

00:27:54.350 --> 00:27:55.910
By using these
services, they've been

00:27:55.910 --> 00:28:00.800
able to run on demand every pull
request, a real linear flow,

00:28:00.800 --> 00:28:03.690
and their contributors
are much happier.

00:28:03.690 --> 00:28:08.070
So, summing it all up, Bazel
makes your build faster

00:28:08.070 --> 00:28:10.590
by maximally reusing
prior results

00:28:10.590 --> 00:28:12.780
and fanning out the
remaining work across all

00:28:12.780 --> 00:28:14.560
of the cores on your machine.

00:28:14.560 --> 00:28:16.920
With GCB, we take it
to the next level,

00:28:16.920 --> 00:28:19.890
globally reusing prior
results and fanning out

00:28:19.890 --> 00:28:22.860
to work across the
cores in the cloud.

00:28:22.860 --> 00:28:24.610
If you're ready to get
started with Bazel,

00:28:24.610 --> 00:28:28.120
there you can find installers
for Mac, Windows, and Linux

00:28:28.120 --> 00:28:31.630
on the Bazel website, as
well as demos, tutorials,

00:28:31.630 --> 00:28:34.420
and great documentation.

00:28:34.420 --> 00:28:36.500
You'll also find references
to the community.

00:28:36.500 --> 00:28:38.350
We've got a mailing
list at Bazel Discuss.

00:28:38.350 --> 00:28:39.850
Very active.

00:28:39.850 --> 00:28:41.432
Stack Overflow communities.

00:28:41.432 --> 00:28:43.390
And we have a conference
coming up in New York.

00:28:43.390 --> 00:28:45.690
This year is our second
annual Bazel conference,

00:28:45.690 --> 00:28:47.650
BazelCon 2018, in October.

00:28:47.650 --> 00:28:51.060
Find information for
that on conf.bazel.build.

00:28:51.060 --> 00:28:53.520
To get started
with Bazel on GCP,

00:28:53.520 --> 00:28:57.960
shoot us an email at
rbe-inquiries@google.com.

00:28:57.960 --> 00:29:00.930
Again, that's
rbe-inquiries@google.com.

00:29:00.930 --> 00:29:04.374
We have found from our own
testing "inquiries" is actually

00:29:04.374 --> 00:29:05.290
kind of hard to spell.

00:29:05.290 --> 00:29:09.827
So take a picture of
that, and type it exactly.

00:29:09.827 --> 00:29:12.410
Maybe those new G Suite features
you saw will help autocorrect

00:29:12.410 --> 00:29:13.500
it, I'm not sure.

00:29:13.500 --> 00:29:14.710
But please, reach out to us.

00:29:14.710 --> 00:29:16.259
We'd love to learn
about your builds,

00:29:16.259 --> 00:29:18.300
challenges you're having,
and how we can help you

00:29:18.300 --> 00:29:20.852
have faster, more
reliable builds.

00:29:20.852 --> 00:29:23.310
We also have a couple of the
senior engineers from the team

00:29:23.310 --> 00:29:24.329
here in the room today.

00:29:24.329 --> 00:29:25.620
They and I will all be outside.

00:29:25.620 --> 00:29:28.170
We'd would love to talk to you.

00:29:28.170 --> 00:29:29.019
That's them.

00:29:29.019 --> 00:29:30.810
Please come, and tell
us what you're up to,

00:29:30.810 --> 00:29:31.740
and ask us questions.

